<h1>Is Converting China Merchants Bank (CMB) Points to Miles Worth It? Beware the "Campaign-Based" Trap</h1>
<p class="lead">Many people search for "CMB points to miles ratio" expecting a single, fixed number. However, CMB's transfer system is rarely a fixed exchange rate—it is driven by limited-time campaigns. If you don't understand this, any calculation you do on whether a transfer is "worth it" will be fundamentally flawed from the start.</p>

<h2>Why You Can't Find a Fixed Transfer Ratio</h2>
<p>Transferring credit card points to external airline or hotel loyalty programs is a negotiated partnership between CMB and the respective travel brands. These transfers usually launch as "campaigns" featuring a specific start and end date, a minimum transfer threshold (e.g., minimum 2,000 points to transfer), a total quota limit (e.g., the entire campaign is capped at 200,000 points across all customers, first-come-first-served), and sometimes a maximum transfer cap per cardholder.</p>
<p>This means the exact same person might get a completely different transfer ratio in March than they do in September. Even within the same campaign period, once the total quota is exhausted, you won't be able to transfer at all. Therefore, asking "what is the CMB points transfer ratio?" does not have a definitive, long-term answer.</p>

<h2>Three Things to Check Before Transferring</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Is the campaign still active?</strong> — CMB periodically launches new rounds of transfer campaigns. When an old campaign ends, the terms for the next one might completely change.</li>
  <li><strong>Is there still quota left?</strong> — A theoretical ratio doesn't mean you can actually get it if the campaign's total limit has been reached.</li>
  <li><strong>Does your specific card meet the minimum threshold?</strong> — Different card tiers (Standard, Platinum, Centurion) often have vastly different transfer caps and minimum requirements.</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout">
  Taking historical CMB campaigns as an example, we've seen combinations of terms like "Minimum transfer of 2,000 points," "Total campaign limit of 200,000 points (first-come-first-served)," and "Maximum of 20,000 points per primary cardholder." The exact numbers change with every campaign. Always verify the currently active terms on the official CMB Credit Card Center website before initiating a transfer.
</div>

<h2>How to Tell if It's Actually Worth It</h2>
<p>Since the ratio fluctuates, the only reliable approach is to take the actual transfer ratio offered by the current active campaign and compare it against the real-world value of the airline miles or hotel points you intend to redeem. Don't rely on gut feeling.</p>
<p>In other words, rather than memorizing a "standard ratio" that might expire tomorrow, build a habit of recalculating the math every time a new campaign launches.</p>

